WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.420
A startup ditched its ai add tool for real people, and the numbers show why that matters for every scroll you do. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 146. A company called Icon once raised money to build an ai system that would take any website link and turn it into dozens of short video ads. Those videos would show fake people on screen, generated entirely by code. The plan felt like the perfect match for social media feeds that never stop moving.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:59.400
Think of it like having a vending machine that spits out fresh ads the moment you press a button. After some quiet time, the team returned with a new product called the Human AdMaker. Instead of letting the ai run everything, they now hire real creators, write scripts together, coach the filming, and deliver finished ads. The results surprised them. Even though the human version costs more to make, it leads to far more sales because viewers trust it more. Picture your own feed where you can see it.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:26.340
Picture your scroll past stiff, too perfect clips that feel like they came from a machine. This shift shows that for some jobs, ai works best when a person stays in charge of the final step, like a chef who uses a food processor but still tastes the dish before serving it. For you, that means the ads you see might start feeling more real again. It also hints at future careers where knowing how to guide ai instead of letting it run alone becomes a valuable skill.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:43.440
If you make videos for school projects or a small online shop, this story is a reminder that mixing your own ideas with ai tools often beats pure automation. You can try this idea yourself by taking a short video on your phone and editing it with a free app like CapCut.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:54.720
Then compare how it feels next to something made entirely by an ai image or video tool. The difference in how natural it feels can teach you a lot about when human judgment still matters.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:07.200
Now let's look at how our brains decide what counts as learning in the first place. You know how you sort your stuff into groups without even thinking, like putting your phone in the electronics pile and your hoodie in the clothes pile?

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:18.200
Imagine you do the same thing with big ideas like learning. Most people picture learning as something slow, curious and a little messy, like a kid figuring out why a bike stays up.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:30.440
When they hear the word ai, their brain quickly puts it in a different pile labeled machine, not alive, just code. That quick sorting happens before they even look at what the ai actually does.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:41.660
So even when an ai spots a pattern in new situations and uses it to answer something it never saw before, people still say it is not learning because the label was already decided.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:53.000
The tricky part is that the same person can give a definition of learning that perfectly matches what the ai is doing, yet still refuse to call it learning if you ask the questions in the wrong order.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:03:00.900
It is like the brain protects the category it already chose, the way a locked folder on your computer keeps files from mixing with the rest.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:11.340
The honest spot in the middle is admitting you do not know exactly what is happening inside the system, but noticing that treating it like it has some kind of thought changes how it responds.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:20.760
That middle spot feels uncomfortable because schools and companies like clear yes or no answers, but it might be the most accurate way to look at these tools right now.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:27.060
The same way you might test a new recipe by tasting as you go instead of declaring it good or bad before cooking.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:36.300
You can test ideas about ai by watching what it actually does, a phone screen with literally zero borders just appeared in a concept from techno.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:42.560
The display goes all the way to the very edge on every side with no notch, no thin line, nothing.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:49.720
It looks like the picture is floating in your hand with no frame around it, the way a window might look if the glass had no sill.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:55.040
This matters because phone screens keep getting bigger while the body stays the same size.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:04:04.320
Removing the last bit of border changes how apps and videos feel, almost like removing the edges from a notebook page so the writing runs straight to the paper's limit.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:10.880
If you like photography or watching shows on your phone, a screen like this would make everything feel more immersive.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:20.300
You can see what it looks like right now by searching techno zero millimeter bezel concept phone on YouTube and watching the short clip from Marques Brownlee.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.840
It only takes a minute and shows the phone from every angle.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:32.240
Google's Gemini gave clear answers when someone asked how data centers could use less electricity and water while still running chatbots.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:42.020
The suggestions included building centers in cold places so outside air does the cooling for free, like opening windows on a breezy day instead of running an air conditioner.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:52.440
Other ideas were switching to sealed liquid systems that never evaporate water and pairing each center with its own solar or wind power so it does not pull from the local grid.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:05:00.380
These ideas connect directly to your daily life because every time you use a chatbot somewhere a server is working and creating heat.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:07.680
You can try this yourself by opening Gemini or any free chatbot and asking it the same question about data center resources.

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:13.000
See what solutions it suggests and compare them to what you already know about energy use.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:21.760
At the world humanoid robot games some machines ran so fast they set new speed records and a few literally caught fire from the kiffer.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:30.800
The events that looked more like household chores such as folding laundry or carrying boxes turned out to be harder for the robots than the flashy races.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:37.760
It is a fun reminder that making robots helpful in real homes is still a bigger challenge than making them go fast.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:46.300
In one recent case chat GPT helped prevent a planned attack by giving advice that led to the person being stopped before anyone got hurt.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:54.720
Stories like this show the same tools that help with homework can also be used in serious unexpected ways when someone reaches out for help.

